# - With -nosubtests: uses Equalf with %q prefix and tt.name or name, and for Qux (no return) uses "for range tests" not "for _, tt := range"
# - With -named: loop var name, and t.Run(name, ...) and assertions without %q? Actually for named without i/nosubtests, it uses assert.Equal without %q, and t.Run(name)

# For -i flag with testify, should also handle named: uses name var but format includes args, not %q? In earlier named i output, it had "S.Foo(%v)" not "%q. S.Foo(%v)" - so named i does NOT include %q, only args. While named nosubtests includes %q.

# So logic for testify formatting:

# Determine if we need to use Equalf vs Equal:
# - If iFlag or nosubtestsFlag => use Equalf (since need to include extra message)
# - Else => use Equal

# For assertion (error):
# - If iFlag or nosubtestsFlag => include fmt.Sprintf with format
# - Else => just tt.assertion(t, err)

# Format string for Equalf and assertion:

# For function with args:
#   If nosubtestsFlag:
#     If iFlag: format = "%q. Func(%v, ...)" with name var and args
#     Else: format = "%q. Func()" with name var
#   Else (subtests):
#     If iFlag: format = "Func(%v, ...)"
#     Else: no format? Actually when no i and no nosubtests, we use Equal not Equalf, so no format.

# For function without args:
#   If nosubtestsFlag: format = "%q. Func()" with name var
#   Else if iFlag: format = "Func()" (no args)
#   Else: no format (Equal)

# For method, same but prefix includes Type.Method

# For want: For multiple returns, each Equalf uses same format.

# For only error case (no returns, only error): 
#   If iFlag or nosubtests: tt.assertion(t, Call, fmt.Sprintf(...))
#   Else: tt.assertion(t, Call)

# But for only error case with -i and no args: format "Func()" as we saw for Baz? Actually Baz has returns, not only error. For Qux (no returns) we have no assertion.

# Let's implement genTestify anew.

# We'll replace entire file again with corrected version that includes proper genDefault and genTestify with all flags.

